WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.105
Your slowest requests got slower.

2
00:00:05.105 --> 00:00:08.482
Is the garbage collector stopping your threads?

3
00:00:08.482 --> 00:00:12.865
Turn on -Xlog:gc, and the JVM prints one line per collection.

4
00:00:13.467 --> 00:00:17.002
Read one line as an event on a timeline.

5
00:00:17.002 --> 00:00:21.245
It happened 0.128 seconds after the JVM started.

6
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:23.449
Collection zero, a young pause.

7
00:00:23.449 --> 00:00:26.424
Pause means every application thread stopped.

8
00:00:26.424 --> 00:00:29.399
Normal means only the young part of the heap.

9
00:00:29.533 --> 00:00:31.614
The cause: eden was full.

10
00:00:31.614 --> 00:00:36.608
The heap went from 14 megabytes used to 1, of 256 committed.

11
00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:41.543
And 12.723 milliseconds is how long your threads stood still.

12
00:00:42.333 --> 00:00:45.375
Here are eight real lines from a busier G1 run.

13
00:00:45.375 --> 00:00:47.445
Which ones stopped your threads?

14
00:00:48.867 --> 00:00:53.878
A pause line has Pause right after the GC number, and ends in milliseconds.

15
00:00:53.878 --> 00:00:55.749
It cuts across every thread.

16
00:00:55.933 --> 00:00:58.962
Concurrent Mark Cycle ends in milliseconds too.

17
00:00:58.962 --> 00:01:00.960
But it ran beside your threads.

18
00:01:01.467 --> 00:01:02.767
Three more pauses.

19
00:01:02.767 --> 00:01:09.053
That's six of eight, and the longest is the full GC at the end: almost 47 milliseconds.

20
00:01:09.333 --> 00:01:12.606
And look closer at Full and Evacuation Failure.

21
00:01:12.606 --> 00:01:15.043
A healthy G1 service shows neither.

22
00:01:15.933 --> 00:01:17.554
How big is that heap?

23
00:01:17.554 --> 00:01:24.501
In a container, the JVM reads the memory limit, and with no heap flags it takes 25% of it.

24
00:01:24.667 --> 00:01:28.543
A pod with a 1 GiB limit gets a 256 MB heap.

25
00:01:28.543 --> 00:01:31.187
Why leave three quarters free?

26
00:01:31.933 --> 00:01:34.731
Because the heap isn't the whole process.

27
00:01:34.731 --> 00:01:40.736
Metaspace, code cache, thread stacks, GC bookkeeping and direct buffers live outside it.

28
00:01:40.867 --> 00:01:49.321
With MaxRAMPercentage=75, the same pod gets 768 MB, and the rest still fits.

29
00:01:49.467 --> 00:01:52.120
Now set -Xmx1g, the whole limit.

30
00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:54.856
What happens once the heap fills?

31
00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:01.381
The rest goes past the limit, and the kernel kills the process.

32
00:02:01.381 --> 00:02:04.037
Exit code 137, no OutOfMemoryError.

33
00:02:04.867 --> 00:02:07.824
In production, log to rotating files.

34
00:02:07.824 --> 00:02:12.539
These JVM flags work the same for Java and Kotlin services.

35
00:02:13.267 --> 00:02:14.011
The rule.

36
00:02:14.011 --> 00:02:18.393
Pause after the GC number means your threads stopped.

37
00:02:18.393 --> 00:02:22.609
Size the heap at 50 to 75% of the limit, never 100.

38
00:02:22.733 --> 00:02:23.712
Quick check.

39
00:02:23.712 --> 00:02:25.915
A 4 GiB pod, no heap flags.

40
00:02:25.915 --> 00:02:27.873
How big is the max heap?

41
00:02:30.067 --> 00:02:32.228
1 GiB: a quarter of the limit.
